I","user":{"login":"RedMser","id":5117197,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUxMTcxOTc=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5117197?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser","html_url":"https://github.com/RedMser","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/RedMser/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-06-03T18:25:34Z","updated_at":"2025-06-03T18:42:54Z","body":"Thanks for the link!\r\n\r\nIt got me thinking about whether/how we want to be able to distinguish between `[a=b]` and `[a b]` style parameters. Currently, both would parse the parameters dictionary as `{ \"b\": null }` but if the user were to add more parameters like `[a=b c]` you'd suddenly have to rely on dictionary key order or something to find out which one is the \"first one\"...\r\n\r\nNot sure of a clean solution to this yet, but I'll keep it in mind for next time I get to work on this PR.\r\n\r\nSidenote: In current Godot, this problem does not exist because neither RichTextLabel nor EditorHelp have a tag which supports **both** named and unnamed parameters present at the same time.\r\n","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2936631624/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null